arXiv:2509.11662v1 [cs.CV] 15 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">MindVL: Towards Efficient and Effective Training of Multimodal Large Language Models 
<br class="ltx_break">on Ascend NPUs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Feilong Chen<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:feilong.chen@huawei.com">feilong.chen@huawei.com</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yijiang Liu<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:liuyijiang7@huawei.com">liuyijiang7@huawei.com</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yi Huang<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hao Wang, Miren Tian, Ya-Qi Yu, Minghui Liao, Jihao Wu</span>Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:huangyi98@huawei.com">huangyi98@huawei.com</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Equal contribution    Project Lead
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose MindVL, a multimodal large langauge model trained on Ascend NPUs. Similar to Qwen2.5-VL, MindVL adopts native-resolution Vision Transformers, which enables it to process images at their original variable resolutions. This design avoids the degradation caused by fixed-resolution tiling while preserving fine-grained details and global layouts, which is crucial for visually dense content such as complex charts and diagrams.
To ensure the smooth training of MindVL on Ascend NPUs, we develop Mindspeed-MLLM, a distributed multimodal training framework tailored for Ascend NPUs. To maintain training accuracy, we implement equivalent replacements for certain operators.
MindVL undergoes a three-phase training process, namely the warm-up phase, multitask training phase, and supervised instruction tuning phase, to gradually enhance its capabilities. This process starts with basic visual and multimodal pre-training, followed by large-scale multiask trainging and instruction tuning. We also adopt multimodal data packaging and hybrid parallelism techniques, which significantly improve end-to-end training speed. To further boost model performance, we specifically introduce test-time resolution search and model weight averaging.
Notably, despite using about 1/10 of the training data required by Qwen2.5-VL, MindVL achieves performance on par with Qwen2.5-VL in evaluations of general multimodal understanding and document/table comprehension. Beyond overall scores, MindVL also delivers leading performance in OCR assessments.</p>
    
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.11662v1/benchmark.svg" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/206;" width="471" height="206"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Benchmark performance of MindVL and its counterparts.</figcaption>
</figure>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The field of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib122" title="" class="ltx_ref">122</a>]</cite> has witnessed rapid advancement in recent years, with a wave of powerful models—both open-source and closed-source—emerging to reshape the landscape of multimodal AI applications. A defining architectural characteristic of these state-of-the-art MLLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> is their adoption of a "modality-bridging" design: typically, a lightweight projector module connects pre-trained Vision Transformers (ViTs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>]</cite> with large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">114</a>]</cite>, enabling seamless fusion of visual and textual information. To achieve their impressive capabilities, most existing MLLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite> rely on NVIDIA’s large-scale computing infrastructure, leveraging trillion training tokens to scale model performance. Although these developments have pushed the boundaries of multimodal understanding, two critical gaps remain unaddressed in the current research landscape.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, there has been limited exploration into training high-performance MLLMs on hardware platforms other than NVIDIA GPUs. As the AI hardware ecosystem diversifies, the over-reliance on a single chip architecture restricts the accessibility and scalability of advanced MLLMs, particularly in scenarios where alternative accelerators (e.g., Ascend NPUs) are the primary computing resources. Second, despite the success of data-hungry training paradigms, minimizing training data while maintaining or even improving model performance remains an urgent challenge—this is not only crucial for reducing computational costs and carbon footprints but also for enabling MLLM development in resource-constrained environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these critical gaps, this paper presents MindVL, a high-performance MLLM specifically trained on Ascend NPUs. Drawing inspiration from the architectural strengths of Qwen2.5-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, MindVL adopts a native-resolution vision transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> design, which allows it to process images at their original variable resolutions. This design eliminates the performance degradation caused by fixed-resolution tiling—a common limitation in many MLLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, while preserving fine-grained visual details and global spatial layouts. Such a feature is particularly vital for handling visually dense content, including complex charts, diagrams, and structured documents, where accurate detail retention directly impacts task performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure smooth and efficient training of MindVL on Ascend NPUs, we further propose MindSpeed-MLLM, a distributed multimodal training framework tailored explicitly for the Ascend NPU ecosystem. For model initialization, we leverage Qwen2.5ViT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> as the visual encoder and strong open-source LLMs as the language backbones, ensuring strong baseline capabilities. To maintain training accuracy while adapting to Ascend’s hardware, we implement equivalent replacements for specific operators that are not natively supported on Ascend NPUs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">MindVL undergoes a three-phase training pipeline: warm-up, multitask training, and instruction tuning, to incrementally enhance its multimodal capabilities. Starting with basic visual and cross-modal pre-training, the pipeline progresses to large-scale instruction adjustment, aligning the model with real-world use cases. Additionally, we integrate multimodal data packaging and hybrid parallelism techniques to significantly boost end-to-end training speed. To further optimize performance, we introduce two key strategies: test-time resolution search (to dynamically select optimal image resolutions for inference) and model weight averaging (to stabilize and improve final performance).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our experimental evaluations demonstrate the efficacy of MindVL and MindSpeed-MLLM. Notably, despite using about 1/10 of the training data required by Qwen2.5-VL, As shown in Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Abstract ‣ MindVL: Towards Efficient and Effective Training of Multimodal Large Language Models on Ascend NPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, MindVL achieves comparable performance to Qwen2.5-VL in general multimodal understanding tasks and document/table comprehension. Beyond overall performance parity, MindVL also delivers state-of-the-art results in optical character recognition (OCR) assessments, highlighting its strength in text-rich visual content processing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The contributions of this paper are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MindVL</span>, a multimodal large language model pre-trained and post-trained on Ascend NPU, which demonstrates the effectiveness and efficiency of training multimodal foundation models on Ascend hardware.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MindSpeed-MLLM</span>, a high-performance training framework optimized for efficient multimodal large language model training on Ascend NPUs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce two enhancement techniques: multimodal model weight averaging and test-time resolution search, both of which contribute to improved model performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experimental results show that MindVL outperforms Qwen2.5-VL on mainstream multimodal benchmarks while using only one-tenth of its training data, further validating the efficiency and efficacy of the Ascend-based training paradigm.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Architecture</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The architecture of MindVL bears resemblance to that of Qwen2.5-VL, comprising three core components: a vision encoder, an MLP projector, and a large language model (LLM). The vision encoder natively supports dynamic image resolutions and adopts 2D RoPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>]</cite> for positional encoding, enabling flexible adaptation to images of arbitrary dimensions. Both during training and inference, the height and width of input images are resized to multiples of 28 prior to being fed into the ViT.
The vision encoder processes images by splitting them into patches with a stride of 14, thereby generating a set of image features. To enhance computational efficiency, the architecture first groups spatially adjacent sets of four patch features. These grouped features are then concatenated and fed through a two-layer multi-layer perceptron (MLP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, which projects them into a dimension that aligns with the text embeddings utilized in the LLM. This approach not only reduces computational overhead but also offers a flexible means of dynamically compressing image feature sequences with varying lengths.
For model initialization, we utilize Qwen2.5ViT <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          The weights of Qwen2.5ViT are derived from the Qwen2.5-VL 72B model.</span></span></span> as visual encoder and Qwen3 LLM / DeepSeek V3-0324 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite> as language backbones, choices that ensure robust baseline performance. In contrast, the MLP projector is randomly initialized.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Model Training</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Warm-up Data</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MindVL warm-up corpus contains 256 billion diverse, high-quality tokens. This data is categorized according to target capabilities, and the curation process for each category is detailed in the following subsections.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Generic Image-Text Pairs</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Web-sourced image-text pairs, which include alt texts, captions, and surrounding contextual text, are now available at an unprecedented scale. With billions of examples, they showcase remarkable diversity across visual and textual concepts. However, such data is inherently noisy, often containing texts that are irrelevant or factually inaccurate relative to the corresponding images, and it frequently exhibits class imbalance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we have adopted a series of filtering measures, including image-based criteria, text-based criteria, aesthetic scoring, pornography and violence scoring, watermark scoring, CLIP scoring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, and URL filtering. For data with inadequate image-text relevance, we use a model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> to recapture the images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, to cover more visual concepts and preserve as many types of long-tail visual concepts as possible, we perform clustering on the images within the filtered image-text pairs. We then manually inspect the data in each cluster and map these clusters to the data’s category labels. For clusters that represent overly broad concepts, we conduct re-clustering. Finally, we obtain the results of hierarchical clustering: a larger variance within a cluster indicates greater diversity. We perform data sampling based on variance—clusters with larger variance undergo more extensive sampling, while clusters with smaller variance are sampled less.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Optical Character Recognition (OCR)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OCR data consists of two types, document OCR data and scene OCR data, each following a structured processing workflow. For document OCR data, the process starts with acquiring and initially screening source PDF data, then processes PDFs page-by-page via a pipeline to generate image-text pairs and image-text interleaved formats. After extracting these data (with image markers removed from pair data and text-only MD files filtered from interleaved data), rule-based cleaning addresses issues like garbled characters or abnormal spaces. For scene OCR data, pre-processed tar packages are first sent to the Focus OCR model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> to generate OCR results; the same tar packages are then processed by the PaddleOCR model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, with results from both models intersected to form production-line OCR results. In addition to the aforementioned process, we have also calculated the coverage rate of words and characters in OCR text, and conducted targeted supplementation for data with relatively low coverage. In addition, we have also incorporated data such as small-language content, ancient books, and calligraphy works.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.3 </span>Tabel &amp; Chart</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For raw tables, we perform layout detection and content recognition separately. Specifically, we use the RapidTable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite> and LORE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">111</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite> models for layout detection and layout judgment, and PaddleOCR for content recognition. Subsequently, we integrate the results and conduct post-processing for HTML formatting. Finally, we convert the table images into HTML format. Alongside the previously mentioned data, we also collect open data to enhance the ability
to interpret tables and charts. For tables, we use the PubTables-1M dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite>, including both its original English version and a translated Chinese version, to gather table recognition data. For charts, we employ chart-to-table conversion and chart-based QA data from existing datasets, including MMC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> and ChartSFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.4 </span>Visual Grounding</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we perform recaptioning on the crawled and open-source images. We then use the Florence-2-large model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>]</cite> to conduct caption to phrase grounding processing. After obtaining object bounding boxes and region captions, we employ SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> to generate point annotations. In addition, we utilize GrIT-20M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite>, a synthetic caption dataset with additional location labels for major visual elements, to enhance the grounding capability. We incorporate referring and grounding data from UMG-41M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite>. Specifically, each region is randomly assigned to either a referring task or a grounding task. In the referring task, we provide the model with a bounding box to generate a caption for that specific region, while in the grounding task, we reverse this by using the caption to predict the corresponding bounding box.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.5 </span>Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics (STEM)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance the model’s disciplinary knowledge, we incorporated a diverse collection of data across various STEM domains, obtained through both crawling and manual annotation. We referred to the disciplinary classifications of MMMU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib119" title="" class="ltx_ref">119</a>]</cite> and K12, extracted such data from a vast volume of PDFs, and also collected publicly available data. Additionally, we used a model to re-annotate the sourced disciplinary images. Ultimately, we obtained nearly 5 million data samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.6 </span>Graphical User Interface (GUI)</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For GUI data processing, we first perform pre-annotation via GPT-4V <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>]</cite>, which involves tasks such as Referring&amp;Grounding (for icons, text, and coitems), ScreenQA, and Navigation. Next, human verification is conducted by human annotators, focusing on Referring-coitem, QA, and the answers for Navigation. After that, a sequence of data post-processing operations is carried out: first, merging with original annotations; second, using UI-Hawk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib123" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite> and GLM-4V <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> to annotate referring-icon, and UI-Hawk to annotate navigation; third, employing GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> to annotate Navigation actions; fourth, merging the results of these three models and performing consistency checks; finally, rewriting the answer style with Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct to ensure consistency and linguistic quality.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Multitask Training Data</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multitask training data consists of Internleaved image-text data, Video, Captions, OCR, GUI, Math, Knowledge, Grounding, STEM and Genreal VQA, totally 179B tokens.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Internleaved Image-Text Data</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For image-text interleaved data, we primarily crawl Chinese webpages, English webpages, news webpages, and Wikipedia from the internet. We then use model-based methods to filter the crawled data. First, we apply CLIP scores to filter the data according to image-text relevance. Next, we task the model with scoring the image-text interleaved data across multiple dimensions, including question-and-answer quality, relevance between images and questions, complementarity between answers, images and questions, and balance of information density. The model is also required to provide justifications for these scores. Finally, we use these scores to filter the image-text interleaved data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Video</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The video data comprises caption and quality assurance data. For differential captioning and QA processing, the preprocessing stage involves segmenting videos into approximately 30-second clips based on shot transitions. Frames are extracted at 1 fps and deduplicated using the CLIP model. These frames are then input to the Qwen2.5-VL 72B model to generate differential captions. Subsequently, the same model is used to produce QA pairs based on these captions. In automated quality inspection, a VLM answers the generated questions, while a LLM evaluates the consistency between producing answers and the original answer The consistency rate serves as a metric for assessing the quality of both QA pairs and captions. Finally, manual inspection involves organizing data by domain, assigning review tasks, and performing sample checks across domains. For overall captioning, the preprocessing and frame extraction steps mirror those of differential captioning, with clips longer than 20 seconds being uniformly sampled to 20 frames. Currently, category labels from sourced data are used as placeholders; model-based labeling will be implemented in later phases. The Qwen2.5-VL 72B model generates general captions, and the Qwen3-4B model automatically evaluates their quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For other types of multitask training data, we have collected a wide range of open source data, as presented in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2.2 Video ‣ 3.2 Multitask Training Data ‣ 3 Model Training ‣ MindVL: Towards Efficient and Effective Training of Multimodal Large Language Models on Ascend NPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Among this dataset, we used a model to reannotate certain portions. Additionally, we also sampled some data from the warm-up phase and incorporated it into the training process at this stage. Furthermore, we utilized language data produced by DeepSeek R1 to preserve the MLLM’s linguistic abilities, with the ratio of multimodal to language data being approximately 8:2 at this stage.</p>
</div>
