<td id="S3.T2.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">512</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">LR warmup ratio</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.1</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.1</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0.1</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Maximum LR</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">2e-4</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">2e-5</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">1e-5</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Minimum LR</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">2e-5</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">1e-5</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">0</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the warm-up phase, only the MLP adapter is trained to connet the vision transformer with the language model, laying a solid foundation for multimodal understanding. The primary data sources during this phase include image captions, visual grounding, OCR and STEM data. These datasets are carefully selected to foster the alignment described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Warm-up Data ‣ 3 Model Training ‣ MindVL: Towards Efficient and Effective Training of Multimodal Large Language Models on Ascend NPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the multitask training phase, all model parameters are unfrozen, and the model is trained on a diverse set of multimodal image and text data to enhance its capacity to process complex visual information and maintain language capabilities. This phase introduces more intricate and reasoning-intensive datasets, such as interleaved data,visual question answering (VQA), multimodal mathematics, agent-based tasks, video understanding, and pure-text datasets. These datasets strengthen the model’s ability to establish deeper connections between visual and linguistic modalities, enabling it to handle increasingly sophisticated tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the supervised fine-tuning phase, all model parameters are unfrozen, and the model aims to bridge the gap between pretrained representations and downstream task requirements through targeted instruction optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We separately pack text data, multimodal multitask data, and web-to-code data, and then combine them using a global batch size-based mixing strategy to ensure balanced learning of both textual and multimodal capabilities. Additionally, we employ within-sample loss averaging to adapt to varying response lengths, preventing the model from developing a strong bias toward either short or long replies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Model Weight Averaging</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Merging weights of deep models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> has been verified to be an efficient way in various applications, including multi-task learning, federated learning, model compression, and continual learning.
We also explore the weight averaging strategies with the models trained under different settings of input sequence length and image resolution between different training phases and in evaluation phase,</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.5.1 </span>Model Merging with Different Training Steps</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MindVL undergoes a three-phase training pipeline: warm-up, multitask learning, and SFT.
In practice, we merge the weights from different training steps in the previous training phase by parameters averaging, then use the merged model weights as the initialization for the corresponding next training phase.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.5.2 </span>Model Merging with Different Training Sequence Lengths</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, for the SFT phase, we train the model with sequence length of 2k, 4k and 8k with max_pixels of 1280*28*28, 3072*28*28 and 4096*28*28 respectively to enhance the model’s adaptability to different context inputs and image resolutions.
Finally, the MindVL model is created by averaging weights of models with different training sequence length.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Test-Time Resolution Search</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluation model is merged by model weights trained with different sequence lengths as well as different max_pixels threshold of training images.
Such search strategy is generally effective as the original resolution of the test images maybe be out of distribution relative to that of the training images.
Therefore, we conduct a grid search about up-scaling small images to surpass a specified min_pixels threshold and down-scaling high-resolution image to be lower than a specified max_pixels threshold.
Specifically, we set the grid search space of the min_pixels as {4, 16, 32, 64}*28*28 and max_pixels as {1280, 2048, 2560,3072,4096,8192}*28 *28.
The analysis results are detailed in Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Results of Test-Time Resolution Search ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ MindVL: Towards Efficient and Effective Training of Multimodal Large Language Models on Ascend NPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Training Infrastructure on Ascend NPUs</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first introduce the diffence between Ascend and Nvidia, and then introduce the development of MindSpeed-MLLM.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Ascend v.s. Nvidia</h3>

<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Comparison Between NVIDIA CUDA and Ascend CANN.</figcaption>
