</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.4 Effectiveness of Model Merging with Different Training Sequence Lengths ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ MindVL: Towards Efficient and Effective Training of Multimodal Large Language Models on Ascend NPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the box plots of the evaluation results using test-time image resolution search strategy as mentioned in Section <a href="#S3.SS6" title="3.6 Test-Time Resolution Search ‣ 3 Model Training ‣ MindVL: Towards Efficient and Effective Training of Multimodal Large Language Models on Ascend NPUs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>.
On OCRBench, DocVQA and InfoVQA, SFT-Merged outperforms other models not only in terms of the highest accuracy scores across different image resolutions, but also shows improvements in both the lowest scores and median scores. On MME dataset, SFT-Merged outerperforms SFT-2K and SFT-8K.
The above results demonstrate that the model weight merging strategy can enhance the model’s robustness against variations in input images of different resolutions.</p>
</div>
