</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the promising progress of MindVL, several limitations remain due to constraints in human resources, data availability, and computational capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational Constraints.</span> Limited computational resources restrictes us to training an 8B dense multimodal large-scale model. We are unable to explore smaller-scale variants suitable for edge devices (e.g., 0.5B, 2B, or 4B parameters), larger architectures (such as 14B, 32B, or 72B models), or Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) structures with varying configurations. However, we are currently investigating an alternative approach: using DeepSeek-V3—a 671B parameter MoE model—as the language backbone to develop MindVL-DeepSeek-V3 on Ascend NPUs. Initial results are highly encouraging: even with only 26B tokens (less than 1/100 of the data used for Qwen2.5-VL), MindVL-DeepSeek-V3 already surpasses Qwen2.5-VL 32B and outperforms Qwen2.5-VL 72B on certain tasks. We will further explore the training efficiency and effectiveness of larger-scale models with increased amounts of training data on Ascend NPUs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Limitations.</span> As a significant amount of data is still undergoing cleaning and annotation, the current version of MindVL was trained on only 447B tokens. We have not yet conducted experiments at the trillion-token scale to explore training stability or further potential improvements. It is worth noting that, of the 447B tokens used, approximately 300B consist of image-pair data, while only 140B are dedicated to instruction-oriented multitask data—a category that is commonly leveraged in the field to push the performance boundaries of multimodal models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Scope.</span> Due to limited manpower, our evaluation of MindVL was primarily conducted on mainstream multimodal understanding benchmarks. We have not yet extended testing to other critical areas such as Grounding, Reasoning, Agent-based tasks, or language-focused benchmarks. A more comprehensive evaluation is planned for subsequent phases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion and Next Steps</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we introduce MindVL, a multimodal large language model pre-trained and post-trained on Ascend NPUs. We provide a detailed exposition of its architecture, data construction, and training strategies. Despite using only one-tenth of the training data employed by Qwen2.5-VL, MindVL achieves superior performance on mainstream multimodal benchmarks. Furthermore, we incorporate techniques such as model weight averaging and test-time resolution search to enhance model performance. We conducted a detailed analysis of the experimental results and derived several interesting conclusions. To enable efficient training of multimodal large language models on Ascend NPUs, we have developed a high-performance training framework called MindSpeed-MLLM. In addtion, we discuss the current limitations of our work. Encouraging preliminary results have been obtained in larger-scale experiments: with only 26B tokens (less than 1/100 of the data used for Qwen2.5-VL), MindVL-DeepSeek-V3 already surpasses Qwen2.5-VL 32B and partially outperforms Qwen2.5-VL 72B. Moving forward, we plan to explore training at larger scales and with more data, conduct more comprehensive evaluations, and further enhance the multimodal capabilities of MindVL-DeepSeek-V3.</p>
</div>
</section>
